From 4b1034c58745f128efcaf24dbd2d16b972d5fe58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Enrico Bartky <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 12:11:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Price a call's prologue, item 4-5; most of its premise is
 wrong

4-4's measurement created this item and told it to do an ablation pass before
building anything. The pass falsifies most of what the item was written to
attack, which is the point of doing it first.

--call-prologue-probe prices each piece against the same empty loop, 200 M
iterations, six rotated repetitions. Five nested `using` scopes cost 0.011 ns.
A try/catch/finally is 0.73 ns, a delegate invoke 0.68 ns, a ThreadStatic read
0.31 ns. The prologue's scopes and bookkeeping - the whole of what 4-5 was
supposed to reduce - are free, which confirms item 2-6 directly instead of by
subtraction.

The one real cost is an AsyncLocal<bool> read at 7.0 ns, and JSEngine documents
it as the opposite: "An AsyncLocal SET is expensive though ... Reads are cheap".
The set half is right and the write-only-on-transition design follows from it.
The read half was asserted, never measured, and is wrong by 24x - and it is the
half that runs on every call, because the scope must save the previous value
before it can decide whether anything changed.

Fixed with the pattern the same file already uses for JSEngine.Current: the
AsyncLocal stays as the mechanism that carries strictness across a suspension -
its stated reason for existing is correct - and a ThreadStatic mirror answers
the reads, kept in step by the AsyncLocal's change handler. 7.0 ns to 0.31 ns,
once per call, which is 43 ms of a 19 694 ms driver: 0.22%.

StrictModeMirrorTests, 9 cases. The ones that matter are the suspensions: a
strict async body still throws after its await, a sloppy one still does not, two
async bodies of opposite strictness interleave without leaking, and a strict
generator stays strict across a yield. Those are what a bare ThreadStatic would
get wrong. Every case also passes on the unmodified engine, so they are a
regression guard rather than a fit to the change.

Everything priced sums to about 10 ns of the ~142 ns a zero-argument call costs.
Allocation adds a little more and is exact: 32 bytes per argument and 32 for the
return, one boxed number each. That still leaves ~85% of a call's fixed cost
unattributable from outside the engine, so the rest of this item is blocked on a
sampling profiler rather than on a design.

And the control every probe in the roadmap has used turned out not to be a floor.
The same counted loop with a literal bound instead of a parameter one runs at
8.36 ns and 0 bytes an iteration against 33.77 ns and 32 bytes, computing the
identical answer - 4.0x and a box per iteration, because a parameter cannot reach
the numeric tier (item 3-3's one acknowledged gap) so `i < n` compares a raw
double against a JSValue. Copying the parameter into a local does not help; the
local inherits its unknown type. The allocation difference is the solid half: a
literal bound also gives the JIT a constant trip count, so part of the 4.0x could
be unrolling, but 32 B to 0 B cannot be.

`for (var i = 0; i < n; i++)` is the corpus's commonest shape, so that becomes
new item 3-5, and on this evidence it is worth more than anything left in phase
4.

Adds CallPrologueMetrics, --call-prologue-probe, and per-shape allocation to
--inlining-call-probe. Repository suite: 7 839 tests across 13 projects, 0
failures.
